    "answer": {
      "short": "Equal products do not imply equal individual factors. The null factor law applies only when the product is zero, so line (3) does not follow.",
      "worked": "From $x(x+4)=2\\times3$, we cannot equate individual factors. The null factor law requires a product equal to zero. For example, the claimed value $x=2$ gives $x^2+4x=12$, not $6$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
